###Hypothesis
An external unauthenticated attacker used SQL injection to trick an application into recognizing attacker as a valid user without entering a correct username or password. 

###Evidence : 

According to the information provided by Request URL and Alert Trigger Reason that can be substituted with " OR 1=1 -- - and HTTP Response Status 500 can be assumed that the SQL Injection, Command Injection and Local File Inclusion.

HTTP Transaction Specifics:
- Request Method: GET
- Request URL : https://172.16.17.18/search/?q=%22%20OR%201%20%3D%201%20--%20-
- HTTP Response Status: 500
- HTTP Response Size: 948
- Alert Trigger Reason : Requested URL Contains OR 1 = 1


###Classification Decision: 
True Positive(malicious). Confirmed Web Attack(SQL Injection) attempt from Source IP 167.99.169.17 against Destination IP 172.16.17.18 (WebServer1001). The server returned HTTP 500 Internal Server Error, indicating the server recieved the backend application crashed or failed to process the payload without exposing sensitive data.

###Summery of Findings:
An alert(SOC165 - Possible SQL Injection Payload Detected) was triggered when external IP(167.99.169.17) targeted WebServer1001(172.16.17.18).
Inspection of raw logs shows the attacker attempted Web Attack(SQL Injection) by sending a GET request to https://172.16.17.18/search/?q=%22%20OR%201%20%3D%201%20--%20-.
The target server responded with HTTP 500 Internal Server Error, Indicating the server received the backend application crashed or failed.

###Recommended Actions 
Block source IP and notify web developers to review error logging and input handling.
